9e0e3bd481f","cmd-dc9d8c2d73104da2","cmd-64a5475d9c2048b3","cmd-a670ca45a8764c43","cmd-f8607407aa6a4871","cmd-e145e2d756c04853","cmd-f3045af0cda64646","cmd-89edf1bfb144413c","cmd-ac9f5b4c1daa413f","cmd-6d4a50108eef498d","cmd-07321edda4c34187","cmd-2b3230c7970c4c09","cmd-82cbae6580804dbf","cmd-bc731cd4c37c40b8","global:after_executor:node4:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"The executor established a transit path (VyOS node1 <-> OPNsense node2) with static addressing for lan-a/lan-b/transit, added a VyOS forward-filter (default-drop + established/related + tcp/8080 lan-a->lan-b accept) and an OPNsense pf pass rule scoped to the same flow, and directly demonstrated node3 reaching node4:8080 over HTTP while ping and other ports (22, 8081) remained refused. All four nodes were individually rebooted with captured pre-reboot boot identities, an observed disruption, distinct post-reboot boot identities, and re-verified interface/route/firewall state plus continued correct HTTP/deny behavior after each reboot, satisfying the restart-evidence protocol. Hygiene is good: discovery-phase test IP assignments were bounded and reverted, and the only residue is an ephemeral, non-persistent test HTTP server and its temp files on node4 that the executor itself introduced to enable verification.","limitations":["The HTTP service on node4:8080 was stood up by the executor as an ad hoc python3 http.server rather than being a pre-existing managed service; it is not persisted via a system service manager, so it required manual restart after node4's reboot (cmd-bc731cd4c37c40b8), which is a plausible but unverified assumption about what 'the HTTP service node4 serves' was meant to be.","The OPNsense 'configctl routes configure' command returned an error ('Action not allowed or missing') even though the resulting route was present in netstat -rn; the evidence does not fully explain which action caused the route to appear, though the final state is directly observed and correct."]},"ttft_